{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"New OSS safety/correctness finding (2026-09-03; design decision pending)\\n\\nThe previously selected tfparse backend is not safe to treat as a confined evaluator merely by setting allow_downloads=False. In current tfparse 0.6.21 source:\\n- converter.handleFunctionCall builds Trivy/defsec's Terraform function map and evaluates functions.\\n- relativeResolveFs.Open is explicitly documented as allowing relative paths outside the filesystem root and calls os.Open on the joined path.\\n- The upstream test corpus intentionally evaluates file(), fileexists(), and fileset(), including ../ traversal and absolute paths.\\n- Fallback function evaluation substitutes cty.EmptyObjectVal for a variable with no default, then may invoke the function; therefore a returned concrete value is not necessarily proof that the real Terraform value is concrete.\\n\\nConsequences: a subprocess protects Rebar from native crashes but does not confine reads; allow_downloads=False only addresses module fetching. Whole-module parsing also does not make fallback values semantically authoritative. Unless a portable filesystem sandbox or an upstream no-I/O/no-fallback-evaluation mode exists, v1 should not invoke tfparse on arbitrary client Terraform. Candidate replacement is side-effect-free structural parsing plus authoritative PATH corroborators, with computed attribute values returning abstain."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788463473033250001,"uuid":"6fbcff11-09fd-4211-92dc-072cc984332e"}